# Both install scripts are read from disk on the client and handed to a shell on
# the remote, so they must keep LF endings even when checked out on Windows.
#
# A CRLF checkout breaks both remotes, differently:
#   - bash rejects every line of the .sh as `$'\r': command not found`
#   - PowerShell sees the .ps1 as a single line, since a bare CR is not a line
#     separator, and fails with `Unexpected token`
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
